  "prediction": "**Seven Men Charged in 2014 Hazing Death, Fraternity Sues for Unauthorized Use of Name**\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, seven men have been charged in connection with the 2014 hazing death of University at Albany student Trevor Duffy. The men, who were allegedly part of a group that used the name Zeta Beta Tau (ZBT) Fraternity, have been accused of forcing Duffy to drink a 60-ounce bottle of vodka, leading to his death.\n\nThe ZBT Fraternity, based in Indianapolis, has released a statement denouncing the group and stating that they were not affiliated with the fraternity. The statement claims that the group was a \"rogue\" organization that used the ZBT name without authorization.\n\nIn response to the allegations, the ZBT Fraternity has announced that it is suing the group of individuals for unauthorized use of its name, symbols, and materials. The fraternity claims that the group falsely represented themselves as members of the Epsilon Gamma chapter of ZBT, which is recognized by the national fraternity and the university.\n\nThe lawsuit alleges that the group's use of the ZBT name and symbols damaged the fraternity's reputation and that they are subject to legal action for unauthorized use of the fraternity's intellectual property.\n\nThe ZBT Fraternity, which was founded in 1898 in New York City as the world's first Jewish fraternity, has chapters on over 90 campuses across North America and has over 130,000 undergraduate and alumni brothers worldwide.\n\nThe case has sparked controversy, with some questioning the motives behind the ZBT Fraternity's decision to sue the group of individuals. Attorney Paul DerOhannesian, who is representing one of the students accused in connection with Duffy's death, has stated that the fraternity's decision to sue is \"novel\" and that he is not sure why they would take that step.\n\nThe charges against the seven men are a significant development in the case, and the lawsuit filed by the ZBT Fraternity is likely to add another layer of complexity to the situation.",
